  Check("scripts", "board-generated", "tests/coord/board-generated.sh", List(), 180000),
  // The ledger is a DERIVED copy of the claim files — every column is a verbatim field — and until
  // 2026-08-10 nothing checked it in either direction. Measured that day: 39 rows against 7 claim
  // files, and each of the 32 extras had a `release-claim` commit. It blocked nobody (the overlap
  // guard reads `.work/active/*.claim`, never rows) and that is why it survived: the damage is a
  // reader believing a path is held. One agent handed work to a "holder" who had released it hours
  // earlier. `--self-test` proves the checker catches each drift on fixtures and then runs the real
  // check, so a green row cannot mean "the checker never looked". Sub-second, no launcher.
  Check("scripts", "ledger-derived", "scripts/coord-ledger", List("--self-test"), 60000),
  Check("scripts", "bench-wrapper-routing", "tests/e2e/bench-wrapper-gate.sh", List(), 120000),
  // `std-import-lanes` and `build-smoke` USED TO BE HERE (added 2026-08-03, moved the same day).
  // Both now run in tier 2 of ci.yml, which is also per-push, so the per-commit answer is unchanged.
  //
  // WHY THEY WENT AND NOT SOMEONE ELSE'S: the suite went over budget twice in one day — 437.9 s,
  // then 425.6 s after a first round of cuts — and these two were the NEWEST things in it. When a
  // shared budget is exhausted, the additions that arrived last leave first; that rule can be
  // applied by their author without a negotiation, which is the only reason anything moved at all
  // while main was red.
  //
  // THE REAL NUMBER, so the next person does not repeat the mistake: two consecutive runs of
  // near-identical content measured 418.6 s and 425.6 s, and the change between them only REMOVED
  // work. Runner variance here is ±14 s, so a cap with less than ~20 s of headroom flaps no matter
  // what is in the suite. The cap is 500 s since 2026-08-04, raised on the project owner's
  // instruction after 420 s went red at 428.8 s with all 58 checks green; that buys headroom for
  // the current suite and answers nothing about what belongs in it. The next cut still has to be
  // structural, not another 10 s shaved off the newest arrival.
  // One file with one `std/` import, three lanes, two locations. `compile-jvm` refused what
  // `run --v1` and `run-js` accepted, because `AutoResolve` consulted `libPath` (the REPO ROOT,
  // where a dev tree has no `std/`) instead of the discovered std root — and the std root itself
  // degenerated to `libPath`, since rule 3 of `discoverStdRoot` was the one rule not filtered by
  // `hasStd`. Four orphaned e2e gates reported it as "the server process EXITED before it
  // listened". Three cells, ~4 s: jvm in-tree and out-of-tree are the subjects, int in-tree is the
  // control that says the fixture is fine. Started at six and was trimmed the same day — at 17.7 s
  // on the runner it was 4% of a budget with 1.4 s of headroom.
  // (BUGS.md compile-jvm-and-std-root-disagree-on-where-std-lives.)
  // `ssc build <src> <dist>` must produce a page byte-identical to `ssc render` of the same source.
  // Orphaned since `d0665660a` and red until 2026-08-03; it passes now and is ~2 s. It compares two
  // exits from ONE pipeline, so it cannot see a failure that empties both — which is why it now
  // also asserts the page contains a `<button>` before diffing. (tests/BUGS.md orphaned-e2e-gates-52.)
  // A program calling its OWN server on the js lane. Two defects at once: the client blocks the
  // main thread in `Atomics.wait` while `serveAsync`'s listener needs that same thread, and the
  // worker source was joined with a literal backslash-n so it never parsed and EVERY outbound
  // request returned `status 0 / "timeout"` for six weeks. Case 4 talks to a LIVE foreign server
  // on purpose: asserting `status=0` from an unserved port — the obvious way to prove the
  // short-circuit stays narrow — is ALSO what a dead client returns, and that is exactly how the
  // second defect stayed invisible. ~3 s. (js/BUGS.md js-worker-source-joined-with-literal-backslash-n.)
  Check("v1/runtime/backend/js", "js-selfcall", "tests/e2e/js-selfcall-gate.sh", List(), 120000),
  // `bench-seed-type` USED TO BE HERE and now runs in tier 2 of ci.yml, which is also per-push.
  // It was the single most expensive check in this suite — 45.4 s of a 420 s budget the suite had
  // just breached (433.0 s, every check green, the run red, `smoke-suite-over-its-own-budget`).
  // Moved by its own author rather than by whoever noticed: what it gates is the bench WRAPPER, so
  // a regression costs a measurement table, not a build, and 29% of a signal designed to take
  // ~157 s is out of proportion to that. Measured per lane before deciding — ssc 2.2 s, js 2.2 s,
  // jvm 11.6 s locally: the jvm cells are 72% of it, and they are not separable from the property
  // because the jvm wrapper is where the defect was. So the choice was WHERE it runs, not whether.
  // Coverage is unchanged; only the budget it spends is.
  // `bugs-report` is the ONLY sanctioned way to ask what is open — grepping the prose is banned
  // because hand-rolled queries disagreed with each other. That makes its blind spots expensive:
  // 118 entries say `fixed` in their heading and `unknown` in their header, so the canonical
  // answer was low by 118 with nothing on screen to say so. The report now prints that deficit;
  // this keeps the DETECTOR alive (planted fixtures, not a frozen count, which every migration
  // would falsify). <1 s.
  Check("scripts", "bugs-status-drift", "tests/e2e/bugs-status-drift-gate.sh", List(), 60000),
  Check("scripts", "release-installer-launcher", "tests/e2e/release-installer-launcher.sh", List(), 180000),
  Check("scripts", "build-ram-guards-guard", "tests/e2e/build-ram-budget-gate.sh", List(), 180000),

  // ── three gates that existed and were run by nobody ────────────────────────────────────────────
  //
  // Each shipped with a working `--self-test` and then sat unwired, which is the same state as not
  // existing: an unrun gate is a hypothesis about the future, not a check on the present. MEASURED
  // before adding, because this suite's budget is the thing it exists to protect —
  // 128 + 72 + 782 ms plain (self-tests add 200 ms / 400 ms / 1.5 s), against a green run's
  // 259.3 s of 330 s. Roughly 1 s spent, ~68 s of headroom left.
  //
  // `--self-test` is on for the two that are cheap and OFF for the submodule one: its self-test
  // makes two extra network round-trips, and a push-path check should not spend a second proving
  // itself on every commit. Its refusal path is mutation-proven in the commit that added it (the
  // 2026-07-30 outage state reproduced, exit 1) — that evidence does not need re-earning per push.
  Check(".", "inbox-queue", "tests/e2e/inbox-gate.sh", List("--self-test"), 60000),
  // The queue has TWO tools and only one was gated. `inbox-route`'s self-test routes a real report
  // in a scratch repo and asserts the reporter fields ARRIVED — a refusals-only self-test passes on
  // a tool that refuses everything, which is a mistake this repository has made before. 352 ms
  // measured, against a 420 s budget.
  Check(".", "inbox-route", "scripts/inbox-route", List("--self-test"), 60000),
  // An import `build-rust` cannot resolve used to contribute NOTHING and still exit 0 — an absolute
  // path throws inside the resolver, the throw was caught, and the user met the consequence as a
  // rustc error in generated code they never wrote. Every other lane already refused the same
  // program. On the PUSH path rather than in ci.yml because it costs no cargo build: four
  // emit-rust runs, ~20 s measured. The last two rows are anti-rows — a resolvable relative import
  // and a program with no imports must still emit, or the refusal has been widened into working
  // programs. (rust-absolute-import-path-inlines-nothing.)
  Check(".", "absolute-import-resolves", "tests/e2e/absolute-import-resolves-gate.sh", List(), 120000),
  // The shared boards had no guard at all — claim-scope and overlap both exempt them by design, and
  // that is how a commit about jsLiteral deleted 65 lines of v3/BACKLOG.md. Its self-test drives
  // the four directions the rule has to tell apart, including the one that caught a defect in the
  // rule itself: a DATE in a heading became the key, so a correctly-worded deletion was refused.
  Check(".", "board-deletion", ".githooks/commit-msg", List("--self-test"), 60000),
  Check("v1/runtime/backend/js", "json-number-policy-js", "tests/e2e/json-number-policy-js-gate.sh", List(), 60000),
  // JsGen has TWO function-emission paths and self-TCO was on only one of them, so the SAME
  // tail-recursive function looped at top level and recursed for real inside an `object` — a
  // difference no output comparison can see until a corpus case happens to get deep enough, which
  // is how it surfaced (scljet-large-page, `RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded`). The
  // gate asserts BOTH placements and asserts the emitted LOOP, not only the answer: a future change
  // that dropped the transform and raised the stack instead would otherwise read as green.
  Check("v1/runtime/backend/js", "js-self-tco", "tests/e2e/js-self-tco-gate.sh", List(), 180000),
  // An intrinsic in the table but not in JsGen's chunk-trigger scan emits its CALL and none of its
  // DEFINITION — `ReferenceError` at runtime, nothing at compile time. THIRTEEN of the twenty-one
  // WsServer intrinsics were in that state on 2026-08-02 and only one had ever surfaced. Derived
  // from the intrinsic table and the chunk source rather than restated, so the next addition to
  // ws-server.mjs cannot quietly skip the list. Pure text analysis, no launcher, ~0 s.
  Check("v1/runtime/backend/js", "js-capability-triggers", "tests/e2e/js-capability-triggers-gate.sh", List(), 60000),
  // Tree-shaking runs on `emit-js`, NOT on the `run-js` the conformance runner uses, so a corpus
  // case cannot see this one: it printed the side effect before the fix and after it. The gate
  // emits the bundle and runs it, and also asserts that a genuinely PURE unused binding is still
  // dropped, so a "fix" that merely stopped shaking fails it. (js-unused-val-drops-side-effecting-call)
  Check("v1/runtime/backend/js", "js-shaker-effectful-binding",
        "tests/e2e/js-shaker-keeps-effectful-binding.sh", List(), 180000),
  // 25 rows, `bin/ssc` as the reference: a char in a NUMERIC context is its code point, and in a
  // TEXT context it is a character. It asserts BOTH sides, which is the point — a fix that widened
  // unconditionally would pass a gate checking only the numeric half while breaking every char
  // comparison in the corpus. (js-char-is-a-plain-string.)
  Check("v1/runtime/backend/js", "js-char-is-a-char", "tests/e2e/js-char-is-a-char.sh", List(), 120000),
  // Runs BOTH v2 fronts and asserts `ssc info --front-report` says F. An F decline is a SILENT
  // downgrade onto the fallback, so a gate that cannot tell the two apart is green either way.
  // (v2-char-is-an-int — the control half: a Char must keep its code point in numeric position.)
  Check("v2", "v2-char-numeric-position",
        "tests/e2e/v2-char-numeric-position.sh", List(), 240000),
  // `case _: Char` across FOUR lanes against `--v1`, which the type-ascription census measured
  // against real Scala on the JVM and which matched on all fourteen types. It refuses to judge at
  // all when `--v1` stops matching that census, so an agreeing pair of wrong lanes cannot read
  // green. Cross-lane by construction — the same question was wrong three different ways at once,
  // twice in the kernel's `__isTag__` and once in JsGen's type-test mapping.
  // (js-char-type-test-cannot-tell-Char-from-String.)
  Check(".", "char-type-test", "tests/e2e/char-type-test.sh", List(), 180000),
  // No method in the v1 tree may exceed HotSpot's HugeMethodLimit (8000 bytecodes) without being on
  // a frozen list — over it, a method is NEVER JIT-compiled, by C1 or C2, and runs interpreted for
  // the life of the process with no warning and no correctness signal.
  //
  // ON THE PUSH PATH, not nightly, and that is the point: this gate existed since 2026-07-30 and had
  // never run once. It was wired to nothing, its census pipeline aborted silently on an empty
  // result, and it scanned target/classes which do not exist after the cache-hit install its own
  // header prescribes. In that silence four frozen methods grew (renderTerm by 3204 bytecodes), two
  // new offenders appeared, and the largest method in the tree was never censused at all because
  // plugin bytecode ships inside a jar nested in a .sscpkg.
  //
  // Its v2 twin sits in ci.yml's `sbt` job, which is `workflow_dispatch` only — I wired this one
  // there first and caught it: next to the twin is not the same as somewhere that runs.
  // ~17s, from 85s: one merged census instead of 56, and an exact class-file size prefilter.
  // --self-test falls through to the census, so this runs the detector's self-check too.
  // (v1-interpreter-hot-path-never-jits.)
  Check(".", "v1-jit-size", "tests/e2e/v1-jit-size.sh", List("--self-test"), 180000),
  // THE TWIN, wired 2026-08-14, and the comment above is why it took this long: it says the v2
  // gate "sits in ci.yml's `sbt` job, which is workflow_dispatch only" — noticed, written down,
  // and left there. What that cost, measured today: `ssc.Prims.methodDispatch8` was 8406
  // bytecodes, over HotSpot's 8000 limit, so the dispatch chain every method call on the v2 lane
  // walks was NEVER JIT-compiled. It surfaced only because I dispatched ci.yml by hand for an
  // unrelated reason. v2 is the lane `ssc run` uses today; its guard belongs where v1's is.
  // ~5 s: it censuses three built modules, no compilation of its own.
  Check(".", "v2-jit-size", "tests/e2e/v2-jit-size.sh", List("--self-test"), 180000),
  // A NEW gate in tests/e2e must be invoked by something. A gate nobody runs reports GREEN by not
  // existing: v1-jit-size.sh sat unwired from 2026-07-30 and in that silence four frozen methods
  // grew, two new offenders appeared, and the largest method in the tree was never censused.
  //
  // A RATCHET, not another sweep. orphaned-e2e-gates-52 drained the pile on 2026-08-02; re-censused
  // 2026-08-13 it was 183 scripts and 36 orphans again, because gates are written faster than they
  // are wired. Today's are frozen by name and the list can only shrink.
  // (orphaned-e2e-gates-52.)
  Check(".", "no-orphan-gates", "tests/e2e/no-orphan-gates.sh", List("--self-test"), 120000),
  // One of the orphans the widened census made visible, drained the same day it was found.
  // It guards the Scala 3 ∩ ScalaScript-v2 RUNTIME subset across uniml core+dialects, and it had
  // been RED and unread: `MarkdownLexer`/`MarkdownBlocks` had drifted back to `StringBuilder` and
  // `Character.toLowerCase`. Fixed on both sides — the buffers took the `Vector[String]` shape
  // `JsonLexer` already ships, and `Character`'s case fold now runs on v2 — so this lands GREEN
  // rather than as a known-red, which is the rule orphaned-e2e-gates-52 paid for.
  // COSTS NOTHING: a `find` plus a `grep` over four directories, no toolchain, no build — measured
  // at 0 s, which is why it goes on the push path rather than into tier 2.
  // Checked NOT to be vacuous: a planted `Character.getType` is still caught.
  // (uniml-markdown-left-the-portable-subset-while-its-guard-ran-nowhere.)
  Check(".", "uniml-portable-subset", "uniml/lint-portable-subset.sh", List(), 60000),
  // A second orphan drained. It reconciles SIX sources for the Core IR node/const inventory — the
  // spec's pinned ```coreir-inventory``` block, `enum Term`, the Reader's accepted heads, the
  // Writer's emitted heads, IrEncode's tags and the ssc0 lowerer — and 10-core-ir.md had already
  // rotted once into claiming "11 nodes" and "Seq is dropped", which is why the gate exists.
  // ZERO TOOLCHAIN (`grep -cE 'scala-cli|bin/ssc|sbt'` = 0) and 0 s, so it belongs on the push path.
  // Its sibling `v2/conformance/coreir-name-guard.sh` does NOT: that one runs the real Writer and
  // Reader through scala-cli, 14 s warm here and a cold compile in CI, so it is tier 2 in ci.yml.
  // NOT VACUOUS, and the first attempt to show that was invalid: renaming the FIRST `LetRec` in the
  // file changed a PROSE mention and the gate stayed green. Scoped to the parsed block —
  // `node  LetRec  letrec  IrLetRec` -> `LetRecXX` — it fails with
  // "10-core-ir.md's node inventory has drifted from CoreIR.scala".
  // (orphan-debt-triage-31-frozen-gates.)
  Check(".", "coreir-inventory", "specs/coreir-inventory-gate.sh", List(), 60000),
  // A third orphan drained. `Lower` carries a hand-written copy of `Exec`'s built-in method names,
  // and the stale direction is the silent one: if `Exec` gains a method `Lower` does not know, an
  // extension with that name is rewritten and SHADOWS the built-in — a wrong answer weeks later, in
  // a different file. This derives the list and compares, so the failure lands on the commit that
  // adds the built-in.
  // 0.14 s and no toolchain. `--self-test` first, and it carries its own plant: it narrows
  // `lower_vocab` to drop one name Exec answers to and requires the check to go RED.
  // THE FALL-THROUGH IS NEW AND WAS THE BUG WORTH CATCHING: `--self-test` used to `exit`, so a
  // suite wiring it would have got the rehearsal and never the answer. Making it fall through
  // exposed that the plant was never undone — the check inherited the narrowed vocabulary and
  // reported RED on a GREEN tree. Both modes are asserted here now.
  // (orphan-debt-triage-31-frozen-gates.)
  Check(".", "v3-extension-vocab", "v3/extension-gate.sh", List("--self-test"), 60000),
  // Three of the 36 frozen orphans, drained. Each was RUN against a fresh launcher (6s, 6s, 7s) and
  // each was checked NOT to be vacuous: re-run with both launcher jars removed, all three go RED.
  // That control found one gate in twenty that stays green with no toolchain at all —
  // f-char-escape-gate, which calls ssc_usable_or_skip and SKIPS rather than fails. Not vacuous,
  // but it reports success while testing nothing, so it belongs only where a launcher is
  // guaranteed. These three have no such guard.
  // (orphaned-e2e-gates-52.)
  Check(".", "info-unknown-flag", "tests/e2e/info-unknown-flag-gate.sh", List(), 60000),
  Check(".", "keyword-import-missing-module", "tests/e2e/keyword-import-missing-module.sh", List(), 60000),
  Check("v1/runtime/backend/js", "js-char-classification-parity", "tests/e2e/js-char-classification-parity.sh", List(), 60000),
  // Mixed Int/Double comparison across FIVE lanes, and the only gate here whose oracle is `run-jvm`
  // rather than `--v1`: on this question the interpreter was one of the WRONG lanes, so the usual
  // shape would have frozen the defect as the reference. It refuses to judge when real Scala stops
  // matching its frozen row — not decoration, it caught a wrong freeze on its first run.
  // Row 8 is a literal pattern (`case 1 =>` against `1.0`): on interp the comparison and the
  // pattern have separate code, and fixing only the first looks complete.
  // (lanes-disagree-on-mixed-numeric-comparison.)
  Check(".", "mixed-numeric-comparison", "tests/e2e/mixed-numeric-comparison-gate.sh", List(), 420000),  // ceiling raised 2026-08-14: costs 59.5s, was under 4x it (smoke-guard-headroom)
  // Network: one `git fetch --depth 1` per gitlink against the submodule's remote. Generous timeout
  // because a slow or flaky network must read as "this run could not tell", not as a repo defect.
  Check("scripts", "submodule-gitlinks-resolve", "tests/e2e/submodule-gitlink-resolves.sh", List(), 120000),
  // Who owns stdin — the program or the CLI. Four launcher starts, ~10 s, and it is worth them: this
  // is the only check that can see the defect at all, because until `std.os.readLine` existed no
  // lane could read stdin and therefore none could notice the CLI was eating it. It deliberately
  // pins TODAY's wrong behaviour on the tools route as well as the right one on the default lane —
  // so the S3 flip goes red here and has to be acknowledged in the same commit rather than slipping
  // through as a silent default change.
  // OPTIONAL — it pins TODAY's behaviour on the tools route (the CLI eats piped stdin), which is a
  // thing we have deliberately decided not to change yet. In the default run that assertion would
  // read as the contract; as an opt-in it reads as what it is, a recorded fact awaiting a decision.
  // Run it with SSC_SMOKE_OPTIONAL=1 before touching `Main.scala`'s stdin handling.
  Check("scripts", "stdin-ownership", "tests/e2e/stdin-belongs-to-the-program.sh", List(), 180000,
        optional = true),

  // ── tests/conformance — the product. One pass, several lanes; see `laneModule` for where the
  //    per-lane results are booked.
  // SSC_CONF_WARM_JVM=1 reuses ONE Bloop build server across the slice instead of paying a cold
  // scalac start per case, which is what `ci.yml`'s conformance job already does for the same reason.
  // MEASURED when it was added: 74.4 s -> 43.9 s locally (-41%). Same `scala-cli run`, warm compiler;
  // correctness is unchanged.
  // `--server=false` on the PARENT scala-cli, and it is a CORRECTNESS fix, not a speed one.
  // MEASURED: six `workflow_dispatch` runs of ONE commit (cf3fad14f) gave 3 green, 3 red — identical
  // code, a 50 % flake, and the log named it:
  //     Starting compilation server
  //     Exception in thread "main" java.util.concurrent.TimeoutException: Future timed out after [30 seconds]
  //       at bloop.rifle.internal.Operations$.about(Operations.scala:529)
  // ── ONE check, four lanes, ~9 s — replacing three that cost 62.9 s ────────────────────────────
  //
  //   corpus-breadth-slice     13 cases over int,js,v2      14.9 s
  //   corpus-jvm-lane           4 cases over jvm            17.8 s
  //   conformance-lanes-flag    the gate on the two above   30.2 s
  //
  // Those were sized for a world where smoke was the ONLY per-push verdict, so a corpus SAMPLE here
  // was the only corpus signal that existed. Since 2026-08-01 tier 2 (`ci.yml` on push) runs the
  // WHOLE corpus — 353 cases, every lane — on the same push, so sampling 17 of them buys nothing.
  //
  // NOT simply deleted, and this is the part worth reading before "simplifying" further:
  // `v1/runtime/backend/interpreter`, `v1/runtime/backend/jvm` and `v2` have NO OTHER CHECK in this
  // suite. Their entire smoke coverage is the corpus lanes. Removing the three outright would not
  // have dropped a duplicate, it would have gone dark on the interpreter, the JVM backend and v2 —
  // locally, before the push, where tier 2 does not exist. So: keep the breadth, drop the sampling.
  //
  // The name still starts with `corpus-` on purpose: the per-lane rollup below reads EVERY
  // `corpus-*` outcome, and that is how int/js/jvm/v2 get attributed to their own modules.
  //
  // `conformance-lanes-flag` went with them because it was never independent coverage — it existed
  // to stop the two checks above failing SILENTLY (a zero-match `--only` exits 0 over nothing; a
  // `--lanes` value could be eaten as the positional corpus dir). That protection is INLINED into
  // the new script, which asserts the case count and every lane BY NAME. Verified both ways: a
  // zero-match pattern and a case declaring fewer lanes each turn it red.
  Check("tests/conformance", "corpus-lane-breadth", "tests/e2e/smoke-lane-breadth.sh", List(), 300000)
)

// ── The module hierarchy, read rather than restated ───────────────────────────

def tsvRows(): List[List[String]] =
  readFile("tests/fixtures/modules.tsv").split("\n")
    .filter(line => line.length > 0 && !line.startsWith("#"))
    .map(line => line.split("\t"))

def declaredModules(): List[String] = tsvRows().map(row => row(0))

// ── The per-check baseline, measured rather than fitted ───────────────────────
//
// `tests/smoke-baseline.tsv` is what this suite COSTS, one row per check, normalised to a reference
// host and regenerated from real CI runs by `tests/smoke-baseline-harvest.sh`.
//
// It exists because the constant it replaces was not one. `436` was the cost of the 78 checks this
// suite had on 2026-08-10 written as a number, so every check registered since ate the margin until
// the cap had to be raised again — 420, 500, 600, 750, four times, each buying a few weeks.
//
// A COUNT would not have worked, and that is measured rather than argued: the 79th check registered,
// `launcher-set-complete`, costs 0.0 s. Scaling the budget by the NUMBER of checks would have
// credited it the average, 8.5 s — 164x. Nine of 79 cost under 0.05 s while one costs 126 s.
//
// Columns are INTEGER — tenths of a second, basis points of the run — because the alternative is a
// hand-rolled decimal parser here, and `split(".")` is a coin-flip on whether the separator is read
// as a regex. 1260 is 126.0 s; 1864 is 18.64 %.
// `SSC_SMOKE_BASELINE` points the reader at another table. It exists for the gate: the budget is now
// data-driven, and a rule whose input cannot be substituted can only be tested by editing the file
// the whole repository shares.
def baselinePath(): String =
  val custom = envOrElse("SSC_SMOKE_BASELINE", "").trim()
  if custom != "" then custom else "tests/smoke-baseline.tsv"

def baselineRowsAt(path: String): List[List[String]] =
  if !exists(path) then List()
  else
    readFile(path).split("\n")
      .filter(line => line.length > 0 && !line.startsWith("#"))
      .map(line => line.split("\t"))
      .filter(row => row.length >= 3)

def baselineRows(): List[List[String]] = baselineRowsAt(baselinePath())

def baselineField(rows: List[List[String]], name: String, col: Int): Int =
  val hit = rows.filter(row => row(0) == name)
  if hit.length == 0 then -1 else hit(0)(col).toInt()

// run.sc prints one verdict line per lane, labelled `[INT]`, `[JS ]`, `[JS/v2]`, `[JVM]`,
// `[JVM/v2]`, `[V2 ]`. Book each against the module that owns that backend. The v2-front lanes
// (`JS/v2`, `JVM/v2`, `V2`) all belong to `v2` — the thing under test there is the front, not the
// backend it emits for.
def laneModule(label: String): String =
  val lane = label.trim
  if lane == "INT" then "v1/runtime/backend/interpreter"
  else if lane == "JS" then "v1/runtime/backend/js"
  else if lane == "JVM" then "v1/runtime/backend/jvm"
  else "v2"

// ── Formatting ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def deciseconds(nanos: Int): Int = nanos / 100000000

def showSeconds(tenths: Int): String =
  (tenths / 10).toString() + "." + (tenths % 10).toString() + "s"

def pad(text: String, width: Int): String =
  var out = text
  while out.length < width do
    out = out + " "
  out

// HEAD and TAIL, not tail alone, and this cost a real diagnosis.
//
// Two CI runs failed on `corpus-breadth-slice` (30606728752, 30606076538). What the log carried was
// eight lines of `at scala.cli.ScalaCli$.main0(...)` — pure JVM plumbing. A stack trace puts its
// CAUSE at the top and its frames at the bottom, so printing the tail printed the least informative
// end of the most important output. The failure was unreadable, and the runner had already thrown
// the rest away: nothing downstream could recover it.
//
// The first lines carry the error message; the last carry the summary. When both are shown the
// omission is stated rather than silent, because "…" with no count reads like formatting.
def headTail(text: String, keep: Int): List[String] =
  val lines = text.split("\n").filter(line => line.trim().length > 0)
  if lines.length <= keep * 2 then lines
  else
    val omitted = lines.length - keep * 2
    lines.take(keep) ++
      List("··· " + omitted.toString() + " line(s) omitted ···") ++
      lines.drop(lines.length - keep)

// ── Running ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

case class Outcome(module: String, name: String, ok: Boolean, exitCode: Int, tenths: Int,
                   stdout: String, stderr: String, skipped: Boolean = false)

// ── Host-speed probe: how fast is THIS runner, independent of what the suite contains ──────────
//
// MEASURED 2026-07-31, and it overturned the question it was meant to answer. The suite's totals had
// drifted from 194-227 s to 247-362 s against a 420 s cap, which looks like structural growth. Per
// check, between the fastest and slowest of eight green runs:
//
//     conformance-lanes-flag  68.9 -> 96.5   x1.40      launcher-input-digest  9.5 -> 20.5  x2.16
//     corpus-jvm-lane         46.4 -> 62.3   x1.34      policy-single-source   8.9 -> 17.7  x1.99
//     no-test-reaches-cli     16.7 -> 28.1   x1.68      ci-status-guard        2.9 ->  5.7  x1.97
//     …median x1.39 across 14 checks; the three that did NOT move are the ones dominated by waiting
//      rather than by CPU (build-ram-guards-guard x1.02, claim-scope-hierarchy x0.96)
//
// Nothing grew. The RUNNER is up to 1.4x slower on some days, exactly as this file's own budget
// section predicts ("uniform inflation across every check is a loaded host, not a slow suite"). A
// fixed second-count therefore measures the day as much as the suite: tight enough to catch real
// growth means flapping, loose enough not to flap means blind.
//
// The probe is process SPAWNING, not arithmetic, because that is what the suite actually spends its
// time on and what a loaded host actually slows. It is independent of the suite's contents by
// construction — the one property that matters, since deriving host speed from the checks themselves
// would hide a check that grew inside the estimate of how slow the host is.
//
// IT CHANGES NOTHING YET. It prints. The budget stays absolute until there are enough CI samples to
// set a reference honestly — this file's own history records what happens when a cap is fitted to
// local numbers (300 s, guessed from a dev machine, red on the first run).
// MILLISECONDS, and 100 spawns rather than 20. The first version reported deciseconds and did 20
// spawns: it printed `0.1s` and a 1.4x difference in host speed rounds away entirely inside that.
// An instrument that cannot resolve the thing it exists to measure is the same defect as a gate that
// cannot see the thing it guards — caught here only because the number looked implausibly round.
def hostProbeMillis(): Int =
  val startedAt = System.nanoTime()
  var i = 0
  while i < 100 do
    exec("/bin/sh", List("-c", ":"), ProcessOptions(None, Map(), Some(10000), true))
    i = i + 1
  (System.nanoTime() - startedAt) / 1000000

// STEP 2 of `scripts/SPRINT.md smoke-budget-drift`, which step 1 left informational on purpose:
// "the budget stays absolute until there are enough CI samples to set a reference honestly".
// Twelve now exist, harvested 2026-08-10 from completed smoke runs — every one at 78 checks, so the
// spread below is host speed and nothing else:
//
//     probe ms   161   166   168   172   184   191   218   218   234   247   259   288
//     suite  s  597.7 695.3 683.4 594.1 669.4 600.2 748.4 775.5 760.3 771.6 743.2 755.5
//
// Least squares: suite ≈ 435.7 + 1.263 × probe. r² = 0.56 — the probe explains just over half the
// spread, so the margin below is NOT decoration; residuals run −77 s to +64 s.
//
// WHY RELATIVE WHEN A BIGGER ABSOLUTE WOULD ALSO STOP THE FLAPPING — the comparison that decided it,
// on those twelve runs, none of which was red because a CHECK found a defect:
//
//     absolute 750 (today)   4/12 runs fail      absolute 850          0/12 fail
//     relative, no margin    7/12 fail           relative + 80 s       0/12 fail
//
// Both zero-flap rules cost the same in false reds. They differ in what they let PAST, which is the
// only thing a budget is for: absolute 850 is blind to growth of 74–256 s (median 155), relative+80
// to 16–157 s (median 79). On a fast host the relative threshold is 131 s TIGHTER than 850; on a slow
// one it is 30 s looser. That is the whole point — do not punish a slow day, do catch a grown suite —
// and a fixed second-count cannot do it by construction, as this file's own budget section says.
//
// A probe of 0 falls back to the last pinned absolute, because a budget derived from a broken
// instrument is worse than a stale constant. Probes ABOVE the sampled range are clamped rather than
// extrapolated — see the function.
def budgetFor(probeMillis: Int, pinned: String, baselineSeconds: Int): Int =
  if pinned != "" then pinned.toInt()
  else if probeMillis <= 0 then 850
  else
    // CLAMPED TO THE SAMPLED RANGE, and this is the correction that running it produced. The twelve
    // samples span 161–288 ms; a dev host under sibling builds probed 1289 ms, and the un-clamped fit
    // answered 2144 s — a budget so large it stops being a guard at exactly the moment a bloated
    // suite would hide behind a slow machine. Outside the range the line is EXTRAPOLATION, not
    // measurement, and a guard may never be more generous than its evidence.
    // Anyone who genuinely needs a bigger number on a loaded host has SSC_SMOKE_BUDGET.
    val lo = 161
    val hi = 288
    val clamped = if probeMillis < lo then lo else (if probeMillis > hi then hi else probeMillis)
    if baselineSeconds <= 0 then
      // NO TABLE, or one this build cannot read. Fall back to the constant it replaced rather than
      // to a smaller number: a missing baseline must leave the budget exactly as good as it was, and
      // never tighter than the suite, or a lost file turns main red for everyone.
      val predicted = 436 + (1263 * clamped) / 1000   // the fit, in integer arithmetic
      predicted + 80                                  // the margin the residuals require
    else
      // THE SAME LINE, used as a host FACTOR on what the suite actually contains, so that
      // registering a check raises the budget by THAT check's cost and nobody has to raise a cap.
      //
      // THE MARGIN IS SIZED BY THE RESIDUALS, which is the only criterion that survives the table
      // being refreshed.
      //
      // RAISED 123 -> 250 ON 2026-08-12, because 123 was fitted on a BIASED sample and made `main`
      // red on 72 % of pushes. Two things were wrong and both are measured:
      //
      //   * The harvester took only `success` runs. What most often makes a smoke run unsuccessful
      //     is exceeding the budget derived from this very table, so the slow runs — precisely the
      //     ones a margin exists to cover — were filtered out. Self-reinforcing: low budget, slow
      //     runs fail, only fast runs are harvested, budget stays low. Fixed in the harvester.
      //   * Even unbiased, the model predicts the TYPICAL run almost exactly (median residual
      //     -0.8 s over 16 runs) and misses a tail: three runs came in +220 to +231 s over. Those
      //     runs are slow UNIFORMLY — median per-check ratio 1.72 across 59 checks against the
      //     fastest run, with the three biggest individual blow-ups accounting for 23 s of a 400 s
      //     difference. Host contention, not a grown check. And the probe cannot see it: 222-234 ms,
      //     which is its normal range.
      //
      // So the tail is exactly what a margin is FOR — what the probe cannot capture — and 250 covers
      // it: 0 of 16 runs red, against 3 of 16 at both 123 and 200. A budget that reds three runs in
      // sixteen on host noise is worse than no budget, because it teaches everyone to ignore it.
      //
      // The guard against a GROWN CHECK is not this number. It is the per-check share report below,
      // which is scale-free and unaffected by a uniformly slow host. If the probe is ever taught to
      // see a 1.7x slowdown, this margin should come back down.
      //
      // An earlier draft justified 123 as "it equals the old rule at the reference host". That was
      // true of the table I calibrated against and FALSE two hours later when the table was
      // regenerated from twelve runs instead of nine — a margin defended by a coincidence is a
      // margin that goes stale silently. Against today's suite the derived budget is about 30 s
      // LOOSER than the constant it replaces, and that is not slack: the suite grew 78 -> 80 checks
      // while the constant stayed at 78, which is the whole defect being fixed.
      //
      // What this is NOT is a decision to tighten or loosen the push path. That has been the project
      // owner's call three times and would be theirs again; an agent moving it as a side effect of a
      // refactor is how a cap stops meaning anything. If the number here needs to be stricter, the
      // margin is the knob and the owner turns it.
      //
      // The derived total does NOT predict better than the fitted one — mean absolute error 37.8 s
      // against 34.0 s — because the residual is host variance the probe does not capture (r2 0.56).
      // The win is that growth self-accounts, and that `most expensive checks` can now name a check
      // that grew, which no total-time cap can do.
      val num = 436000 + 1263 * clamped
      val den = 436000 + 1263 * 224                   // the reference host: the clamp's midpoint
      (baselineSeconds * num) / den + 250

def runCheck(check: Check): Outcome =
  val startedAt = System.nanoTime()
  // A command that cannot be STARTED (renamed script, lost +x bit) throws out of `exec`. Left
  // uncaught it kills the runner on check 1 of 10 — non-zero, so not fail-open, but it reports one
  // unexplained line instead of nine other verdicts. Book it as this check's failure and continue.
  val result =
    try exec(check.cmd, check.argv, ProcessOptions(None, check.env, Some(check.timeoutMs), true))
    catch case e => ProcessResult("", "could not run `" + check.cmd + "`: " + e.toString(), 127)
  val elapsed = deciseconds(System.nanoTime() - startedAt)
  Outcome(check.module, check.name, result.exitCode == 0, result.exitCode, elapsed,
          result.stdout, result.stderr)

// A lane tally the harness never printed is NOT zero failures — it is an unreadable harness. The
// corpus check therefore fails when it produced no lane lines at all, rather than reporting a
// silent all-green over nothing (`feedback: pre-judging instead of comparing`).
//
// KNOWN-RED gets its OWN bucket rather than being folded into either side. It is a declared,
// self-expiring exception: counted as a pass it would hide a lane that is red on purpose, and
// counted as a failure it would make the suite permanently red. Left out of both — which is what
// the first version of this parse did — it becomes invisible, which is the worst of the three.
case class LaneTally(module: String, pass: Int, knownRed: Int, fail: Int)

def laneVerdicts(stdout: String): List[List[String]] =
  stdout.split("\n")
    .map(line => line.trim())
    .filter(line => line.startsWith("PASS [") || line.startsWith("FAIL [") ||
                    line.startsWith("DIVERGE [") || line.startsWith("KNOWN-RED ["))
    .map(line =>
      val open = line.indexOf("[")
      val close = line.indexOf("]")
      List(line.substring(0, open).trim(), line.substring(open + 1, close)))

def tallyFor(module: String, verdicts: List[List[String]]): LaneTally =
  val mine = verdicts.filter(v => laneModule(v(1)) == module)
  LaneTally(module,
            mine.filter(v => v(0) == "PASS").length,
            mine.filter(v => v(0) == "KNOWN-RED").length,
            mine.filter(v => v(0) != "PASS" && v(0) != "KNOWN-RED").length)

def showTally(tally: LaneTally): String =
  var text = tally.pass.toString() + " pass"
  if tally.knownRed > 0 then text = text + ", " + tally.knownRed.toString() + " known-red"
  if tally.fail > 0 then text = text + ", " + tally.fail.toString() + " FAIL"
  text

// ── main ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def main() =
  val argv = args
  val listOnly = argv.contains("--list")
  // ABSOLUTE ONLY AS AN OVERRIDE. `SSC_SMOKE_BUDGET` still pins a number when someone wants one;
  // with it unset the budget is derived from the host-speed probe below. See `budgetFor`.
  val budgetOverride = envOrElse("SSC_SMOKE_BUDGET", "").trim()
  val modules = declaredModules()

  // The hierarchy check comes FIRST: a suite that silently drops a check because its module name
  // drifted is worse than no suite, because it still reports green.
  val unknown = suite.filter(check => !modules.contains(check.module))
  if unknown.length > 0 then
    println("smoke-ci: check(s) name a module that is not in tests/fixtures/modules.tsv:")
    unknown.foreach(check => println("  " + check.name + " -> " + check.module))
    println("The module list is the source of module identity. Add the module there, or fix the name.")
    exit(1)

  // `--budget` prints the number and exits. It exists so the budget can be TESTED: the rule is now
  // data-driven, and a property like "a bigger baseline gives a bigger budget" is worth nothing if
  // checking it costs a ten-minute suite run. See tests/e2e/smoke-budget-gate.sh.
  if argv.contains("--budget") then
    // EVERY table in ONE invocation, because booting this toolchain costs 10 s and the gate needs
    // five answers. The first version took a single table and the gate paid 50 s for arithmetic that
    // takes microseconds — measured, after registering it in the suite with a comment claiming
    // "about a second". Each line is <table>TAB<key>TAB<value> so the reader never has to count
    // lines or track block boundaries.
    val probeForBudget = hostProbeMillis()
    val tables = argv.filter(a => a != "--budget")
    val wanted = if tables.length == 0 then List(baselinePath()) else tables
    wanted.foreach(path =>
      val rows = baselineRowsAt(path)
      val seconds =
        suite.map(check => baselineField(rows, check.name, 1))
             .filter(tenths => tenths > 0).foldLeft(0)((a, b) => a + b) / 10
      println(path + "\tbudget\t" + budgetFor(probeForBudget, budgetOverride, seconds).toString())
      println(path + "\tbaseline-rows\t" + rows.length.toString())
      println(path + "\tbaseline-seconds\t" + seconds.toString())
      println(path + "\tprobe-ms\t" + probeForBudget.toString()))
    exit(0)

  if listOnly then
    println("smoke-ci — " + suite.length.toString() + " checks, budget " +
            (if budgetOverride == "" then "host-relative" else budgetOverride + "s"))
    // OPTIONAL IS MARKED HERE, because `--list` is what a reader consults to know what a push runs
    // and an unmarked list overstates it by however many are parked. That was one check when this
    // printer was written and is ten now, so the difference stopped being cosmetic.
    suite.foreach(check =>
      println("  " + pad(check.module, 34) + check.name + "  (" + check.cmd + ")" +
              (if check.optional then "  [optional — SSC_SMOKE_OPTIONAL=1]" else "")))
    exit(0)

  val probeMillis = hostProbeMillis()
  // Summed over the whole suite: a check with no row contributes nothing here and is charged its
  // MEASURED cost at the end instead, so a table that has fallen behind can never cause a red.
  val baseline = baselineRows()
  val baselineSeconds =
    suite.map(check => baselineField(baseline, check.name, 1))
         .filter(tenths => tenths > 0).foldLeft(0)((a, b) => a + b) / 10
  val budgetSeconds = budgetFor(probeMillis, budgetOverride, baselineSeconds)
  println("smoke-ci — " + suite.length.toString() + " checks across " +
          modules.length.toString() + " declared modules, budget " +
          budgetSeconds.toString() + "s, host probe " + probeMillis.toString() +
          "ms (100 process spawns" +
          (if budgetOverride == "" then "; budget derived from it)" else "; budget PINNED by SSC_SMOKE_BUDGET)"))
  if budgetOverride == "" then
    println("           budget from " + baseline.length.toString() + " per-check baselines totalling " +
            baselineSeconds.toString() + "s at the reference host (tests/smoke-baseline.tsv)")
  println("")

  var outcomes = List[Outcome]()
  var lastModule = ""
  suite.foreach(check =>
    if check.module != lastModule then
      println(check.module)
      lastModule = check.module
    val outcome =
      if check.optional && !runOptional then
        // `skipped = true` rather than `ok = true`: counting a check nobody ran as a pass is how a
        // suite reports a bigger number than it earned, and the verdict line below is the one thing
        // a reader takes away.
        Outcome(check.module, check.name, true, 0, 0, "", "", true)
      else runCheck(check)
    val mark =
      if check.optional && !runOptional then "  skip "
      else if outcome.ok then "  ok   " else "  FAIL "
    val suffix =
      if check.optional && !runOptional then "  (optional — SSC_SMOKE_OPTIONAL=1 to run)"
      else showSeconds(outcome.tenths)
    println(mark + pad(outcome.name, 34) + suffix)
    if !outcome.ok then
      // The exit code first: 124 is the timeout guard firing, 127 a command that could not start,
      // 137 a kill (OOM on a runner). Those three are answered by the number alone, and reading
      // them out of the output is guesswork.
      //
      // -1 was NOT in that legend and is what THIS runner reports when its own guard fires, so a
      // killed check printed a bare `exit code -1` with no stdout and no stderr — three facts that
      // look like a crash and are actually a timeout. It cost a wrong diagnosis once already
      // (tests/BUGS.md smoke-check-guards-sized-by-local-time). Say which it is, and say the guard
      // it hit, because "60.1 s against 60 000 ms" is the whole answer and the reader has to be
      // able to see it without opening this file.
      val killed = outcome.exitCode == -1 || outcome.exitCode == 124
      val why =
        if killed then " — TIMED OUT against its " + (check.timeoutMs / 1000).toString() + "s guard"
        else if outcome.exitCode == 127 then " — the command could not start"
        else if outcome.exitCode == 137 then " — killed (OOM on the runner?)"
        else ""
      println("         | exit code " + outcome.exitCode.toString() + why)
      headTail(outcome.stdout, 10).foreach(line => println("         | " + line))
      headTail(outcome.stderr, 10).foreach(line => println("         | " + line))
    outcomes = outcomes ++ List(outcome))

  // ── Per-module rollup: the top-level accounting ─────────────────────────────
  //
  // Two kinds of row, because the suite has two kinds of coverage. Modules with their own gates
  // are booked by check; the backend modules are booked by the corpus lane they own. Both roll up
  // into the single verdict below, which is the number anyone reading a CI log actually needs.
  println("")
  println("by module")
  modules.foreach(module =>
    val mine = outcomes.filter(o => o.module == module)
    if mine.length > 0 then
      val green = mine.filter(o => o.ok).length
      val spent = mine.map(o => o.tenths).foldLeft(0)((a, b) => a + b)
      println("  " + pad(module, 34) + green.toString() + "/" + mine.length.toString() +
              " checks   " + showSeconds(spent)))

  // EVERY corpus check, not just the first. The suite runs two of them — breadth on int/js/v2, and
  // the JVM lane on three cases — and reading only one would drop a whole lane out of the rollup
  // while still printing a confident table. Same invisibility the KNOWN-RED bucket exists to avoid.
  val corpus = outcomes.filter(o => o.name.startsWith("corpus-"))
  var laneReadable = true
  var laneFailures = 0
  if corpus.length > 0 then
    val verdicts =
      corpus.map(o => laneVerdicts(o.stdout)).foldLeft(List[List[String]]())((a, b) => a ++ b)
    if verdicts.length == 0 then
      laneReadable = false
      println("  corpus lanes: UNREADABLE — run.sc printed no `PASS [lane]` lines. The harness")
      println("  output format changed, or the run died before any case reported. Not green.")
    else
      List("v1/runtime/backend/interpreter", "v1/runtime/backend/js",
           "v1/runtime/backend/jvm", "v2").foreach(module =>
        val tally = tallyFor(module, verdicts)
        laneFailures = laneFailures + tally.fail
        if tally.pass + tally.knownRed + tally.fail > 0 then
          println("  " + pad(module, 34) + showTally(tally) + "   (corpus lanes)"))

  // ── Verdict ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  val failed  = outcomes.filter(o => !o.ok)
  val skipped = outcomes.filter(o => o.skipped)
  val ran     = outcomes.filter(o => !o.skipped)
  val totalTenths = outcomes.map(o => o.tenths).foldLeft(0)((a, b) => a + b)
  // The budget FAILS the run on CI and only WARNS locally, and that asymmetry is the point.
  //
  // Its job is to stop the PUSH PATH growing, and the push path is the GitHub job — a dedicated
  // 2-core runner. A developer machine is running several agents' builds at once; measured here at
  // load 19, `ci-status-guard` alone took 72-78 s against 4.0 s for the identical check on a runner.
  // The number being compared is a CI-sized number, so locally the check measures host contention,
  // not the suite.
  //
  // Failing on it locally had a concrete cost: an agent who cannot get a green run before pushing
  // pushes without one. That happened three times in one session — mine — each time with every
  // check green and only the total over. The timings are still printed and the warning is still
  // loud; what changes is that a loaded host no longer blocks the pre-push discipline it exists to
  // support.
  val onCi = envOrElse("CI", "") != ""
  // A CHECK WITH NO BASELINE ROW CANNOT CAUSE A RED. It was not in the table the budget came from,
  // so charging it its own measured cost is the only honest arithmetic — and it is the fail-safe
  // direction: registering a check is never the thing that turns main red for everyone. It is named
  // below instead, which is the action that actually needs taking.
  val unbudgeted = ran.filter(o => baselineField(baseline, o.name, 1) < 0)
  val unbudgetedTenths = unbudgeted.map(o => o.tenths).foldLeft(0)((a, b) => a + b)
  val overBudget = totalTenths > budgetSeconds * 10 + unbudgetedTenths

  println("")
  println("checks: " + (ran.length - failed.length).toString() + "/" +
          ran.length.toString() + " green    " +
          showSeconds(totalTenths) + " of " + budgetSeconds.toString() + "s budget")
  // Never let the skipped ones be inferred from a smaller total. The count AND the names, every
  // run — an optional check and a deleted one must not look the same in a log.
  if skipped.length > 0 then
    println("        " + skipped.length.toString() + " optional check(s) NOT run: " +
            skipped.map(o => o.name).mkString(", ") + "   (SSC_SMOKE_OPTIONAL=1 runs them)")

  // The three most expensive checks, with their SHARE of the run.
  //
  // This file already tells the reader what to look for — "one check that grew is the signal this
  // exists for" — and then leaves them to scan sixty timings for it. On 2026-08-05 that scan found
  // `launcher-input-digest` at 227 s: 27% of the run and 3x the next check, from a script spawning
  // two processes per line over 7272 lines. Fixed, it is 18 s and the suite went 467.6 -> 266.1 s.
  // Finding it took ranking the CI log by hand; printing the ranking costs nothing and is the
  // difference between a number nobody acts on and one that names its own cause.
  //
  // A SHARE rather than a comparison with a previous run, deliberately: the suite keeps no history,
  // and this file's own host-speed probe shows why a raw second-count would mislead — the same
  // runner varies up to 1.4x day to day, so "slower than last time" is usually the host. A share is
  // scale-free: uniform inflation leaves it unchanged, and one check growing moves it.
  val ranked = outcomes.filter(o => !o.skipped).sortBy(o => 0 - o.tenths)
  val totalTenths = outcomes.map(o => o.tenths).foldLeft(0)((a, b) => a + b)
  if ranked.length > 0 && totalTenths > 0 then
    println("")
    println("most expensive checks")
    // AGAINST THE BASELINE SHARE, because the total cannot see this. Measured 2026-08-10: on a median
    // probe the budget is 791 s and the suite is 647.9 s, so DOUBLING the most expensive check
    // reaches 767.5 s and stays green. A check can double without the guard whose stated purpose is
    // catching exactly that saying a word. Its share, meanwhile, moves 18.5 % to 31.2 %.
    //
    // A share and not a second-count, and that is measured too: across nine CI runs the share was
    // the tighter unit for 61 of 78 checks. The suite total's spread is 74.3 s while the per-check
    // spreads in quadrature come to 25.2 s — the signature of a common multiplier, which is host
    // speed, which a share cancels and a second-count does not.
    //
    // THE MARKER IS CI-ONLY, for the same reason the budget only FAILS on CI. The first local run
    // flagged `ci-status-guard` as GREW +9pp: baseline 3.1 s, measured 81.5 s — and this file already
    // records that the identical check takes 4.0 s on a runner and 72-78 s here at load 19. A share
    // cancels UNIFORM inflation; a dev host under other agents' builds does not inflate uniformly,
    // it inflates whatever happens to be spawning processes. Locally the baseline is still printed,
    // so the number is there to read; what is suppressed is the accusation.
    //
    // REPORTED, NOT ENFORCED, and deliberately: the spread of a per-check share across hosts is not
    // yet known, and a new way to turn main red before its false-positive rate is measured is how a
    // gate gets ignored. Sergiy's call on 2026-08-10.
    ranked.take(3).foreach(o =>
      val pct = (o.tenths * 100) / totalTenths
      val wasBp = baselineField(baseline, o.name, 2)
      val nowBp = (o.tenths * 10000) / totalTenths
      println("  " + pad(o.module + " " + o.name, 46) + showSeconds(o.tenths) +
              "   " + pct.toString() + "% of the run" +
              (if wasBp < 0 then "   (no baseline)"
               else "   baseline " + (wasBp / 100).toString() + "%" +
                    (if nowBp - wasBp >= 200 && onCi then "  GREW +" + ((nowBp - wasBp) / 100).toString() + "pp"
                     else ""))))

  if unbudgeted.length > 0 then
    println("")
    println("  " + unbudgeted.length.toString() + " check(s) have no baseline row: " +
            unbudgeted.map(o => o.name).mkString(", "))
    println("  charged their measured " + showSeconds(unbudgetedTenths) +
            " so they cannot fail the run — refresh with tests/smoke-baseline-harvest.sh")

  if failed.length > 0 then
    println("")
    println("FAILED:")
    failed.foreach(o => println("  " + o.module + "  " + o.name))
  if overBudget then
    println("")
    if onCi then
      println("OVER BUDGET — the push path is what this suite exists to keep short. Cut a check or")
      println("make one faster; raising SSC_SMOKE_BUDGET is how the old 13.4-minute path happened.")
      println("Read the per-check timings above: one check that grew is the signal this exists for.")
    else
      println("over budget LOCALLY — reported, not failed. This budget is sized for a dedicated CI")
      println("runner; a dev host running several agents' builds inflates every check (measured at")
      println("load 19: ci-status-guard 78 s here against 4 s on a runner). If ONE check grew rather")
      println("than all of them, that is real and worth chasing before you push.")
  if !laneReadable then
    println("")
    println("CORPUS LANE OUTPUT UNREADABLE — see above.")
  // Independent of run.sc's exit code on purpose. If the harness ever prints a FAIL lane and still
  // exits 0, the check above reads green — a fail-open harness is the one failure mode a smoke
  // suite must not inherit, so the lane lines get their own say in the verdict.
  if laneFailures > 0 then
    println("")
    println("CORPUS LANE FAILURES: " + laneFailures.toString() +
            " — reported by the lane lines even though the check itself did not fail.")

  if failed.length > 0 || (overBudget && onCi) || !laneReadable || laneFailures > 0 then exit(1)
  else exit(0)
```
